<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Palau</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Palau is an island country in the western Pacific, at the western end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caroline-islands">Caroline Islands</a>, made up of about 340 islands. Ruled in turn by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> from 1899 and by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> from 1914, whose South Seas Government sat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koror">Koror</a>, it saw hard fighting at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peleliu">Peleliu</a> in 1944 and then became part of the US Trust Territory. It became independent in 1994 in free association with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/palau.jpg" alt="Palau" width="320" height="201" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Original: John Blau Skebong Vector: Nightstallion · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Palau.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Palau is an archipelago of about 340 islands in the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pacific-ocean">Pacific Ocean</a>, the western part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caroline-islands">Caroline Islands</a>, with a total area of 466 square kilometres. Its most populous islands are <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angaur">Angaur</a>, Babeldaob, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koror">Koror</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peleliu">Peleliu</a>, and about two-thirds of the people live on Koror. North of them lies the coral atoll of Kayangel, and west lie about 200 uninhabited Rock Islands. A remote group of six Southwest Islands, some 604 kilometres away, make up the states of Hatohobei and Sonsorol.</p><p>The Palauan name for the islands, Belau, derives from the word for village, beluu, or from aibebelau, meaning indirect replies, which relates to a creation myth. The name Palau came into English from the Spanish Los Palaos through German. An older English name was the Pelew Islands.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The islands were first settled in the 3rd or 2nd millennium BCE by Austronesian peoples, most likely from what is now the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>. In December 1696 sailors from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caroline-islands">Caroline Islands</a> were stranded on Samar, where the missionary Paul Klein met them. They showed him with pebbles the position and size of their islands, and he drew from this the first European map of the Palau area.</p><p>The islands became part of the Spanish East Indies in 1885. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>'s defeat in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-american-war">Spanish-American War</a> they were sold to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> in 1899 and administered as part of German New Guinea. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> they fell within the Japanese-ruled South Seas Mandate of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a>. In the Second World War American and Japanese troops fought in the Mariana and Palau Islands campaign, including the major Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peleliu">Peleliu</a>.</p><p>In 1947 Palau became part of the United States-governed Trust Territory of the Pacific Islands. A referendum in 1978 rejected joining the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/federated-states-of-micronesia">Federated States of Micronesia</a>, and Palau adopted a constitution in 1981. It gained full <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> in 1994 under a Compact of Free Association with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations-general-assembly">United Nations General Assembly</a> admitted it on 15 December 1994.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Republic of Palau is a presidential republic with a bicameral Palau National Congress. Its capital, Ngerulmud, is on Babeldaob, the largest island, and its most populous city is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koror">Koror</a>. The official languages are Palauan and English, and the currency is the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> dollar. Palauan society follows a strict matrilineal system, seen in funerals, marriages, inheritance and the passing of traditional titles.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Palau” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The writer Agawa Hiroyuki recorded hearing a local guide sing it on Bougainville in 1966; in <b>Palau</b> it lives on with new words, and in 1994 Japanese reporters filmed veterans of Indonesia's war of independence singing it at independence celebrations.</blockquote><a href="/songs/patriotic-march">Patriotic March</a></li><li><blockquote>That year Germany bought the Caroline, <b>Palau</b> and Northern Mariana Islands from Spain, and the Marshall Islands Protectorate, including Nauru, was merged in 1906.</blockquote><a href="/state/german-new-guinea">German New Guinea</a></li><li><blockquote>Early in the First World War the Japanese navy seized Germany's island colonies north of the equator: the Marianas except Guam, the Carolines, <b>Palau</b> and the Marshalls.</blockquote><a href="/state/south-seas-mandate">South Seas Mandate</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Palau's</b> compact took effect in 1994, ending the trusteeship.</blockquote><a href="/state/trust-territory-of-the-pacific-islands">Trust Territory of the Pacific Islands</a></li><li><blockquote>Yapese voyagers quarried huge limestone discs, rai, in <b>Palau</b> and carried them home as stone money, still used in ceremonial exchange.</blockquote><a href="/state/yapese-chiefdoms">Yapese chiefdoms</a></li></ul></section>
